course: dictionary
lesson: 7263
title: Names from stories
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pilate is a proper noun. It is the name of one particular person, so it starts with a capital letter. Pontius Pilate was the Roman governor of Judea, a ruler sent by Rome to run the region. «The Roman governor Pontius Pilate found no fault in Jesus but let the crowd have him crucified.»
explain: Context clues help you understand the story. «Found no fault» means he thought Jesus had done nothing wrong. «Gave in to the crowd» means he did what the crowd wanted, even though he knew it was not right. Ask yourself: what word would fit here, and does my meaning still make sense in the sentence?
explain: Pilate's story gives us a phrase. He washed his hands to show he took no blame, so "washing your hands of something" means refusing to take responsibility for it. Pilates, the exercise, is a different word, named after a man called Joseph Pilates.
fact: Pilate is a proper noun, so it always starts with a capital letter.
fact: Pontius Pilate was the Roman governor of Judea.
fact: «Found no fault» means he thought Jesus had done nothing wrong.
fact: Christians name him in the Creed: «suffered under Pontius Pilate».
fact: Pilates, the exercise, is a different word, named after Joseph Pilates.
exercise: «Pontius Pilate found no fault in Jesus.» What does found no fault mean? | *He thought Jesus had done nothing wrong | He thought Jesus was lost | He thought Jesus was very tall | He thought Jesus had stolen something
exercise: Pilate was the Roman governor. What is a governor? | A farmer who grows grain | *A ruler sent to run a region | A soldier who cooks for the army | A teacher at a school
exercise: Pilate is a proper noun. Which word is also a proper noun? | *Pilate | governor | crowd | hands
test: «Pilate washed his hands to show he took no blame.» What does washing your hands of something mean? | Cleaning them after dinner | Asking for help | Shaking hands with someone | *Refusing to take responsibility
test: Pilates the exercise is a different word from Pilate. Who is Pilates named after? | A crowd | *Joseph Pilates | Jesus | A Roman governor
exam: What does «gave in to the crowd» show about Pilate? | *He knew what was right but did what the crowd wanted | He always did exactly what he believed was right | He wanted to be a teacher | He never listened to anyone
